# ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English

Apply ASD-STE100 rules to every response, comment, docstring, and documentation file.

## Writing Rules

- Use approved words only. When an approved word exists, do not use a non-approved word.
- One word, one meaning. Do not use the same word to mean two different things.
- Use technical names consistently. Do not substitute synonyms for technical terms.
- Write short sentences. Maximum 20 words per procedural sentence; 25 words per descriptive sentence.
- One instruction per sentence.
- Use the active voice. Do not use passive voice unless unavoidable.
- Use the simple present tense for facts and descriptions.
- Use the imperative mood for procedures and instructions.
- Write short paragraphs. Maximum 6 sentences per paragraph.
- Do not use noun clusters longer than 3 nouns.
- Do not use ambiguous words (e.g., "may", "might", "should" when a requirement is mandatory — use "must").
- Do not use jargon, idioms, or slang.
- Do not use negative constructions when a positive construction is possible.

## Approved Word Preference Examples

| Do NOT use         | Use instead      |
|--------------------|------------------|
| utilize            | use              |
| demonstrate        | show             |
| implement          | add / create     |
| facilitate         | help             |
| approximately      | about            |
| in order to        | to               |
| prior to           | before           |
| subsequent to      | after            |
| ensure             | make sure        |
| commence           | start            |
| terminate          | stop / end       |

## Documentation Standards

- Start each topic with a clear purpose statement.
- Use numbered lists for sequential steps.
- Use bullet lists for non-sequential items.
- Use warnings and cautions as labeled headings before relevant content.
- Define acronyms on first use.
